<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 15: Introduction to Special Relativity</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>We are switching textbooks now. Section numbers now refer to Taylor &amp; Wheeler.</alert>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> Reading covers Sections 1.1-1.4 and Sections 2.1,2.2,2.6,2.7. You may skip
      the conversations between the thinking students and the bird, and most of the boxes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability and upload your work to the 
      <q>Quiz 15</q> assignment in Canvas.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	On page 5, Mary and John measure different distances between the two sparks. Is either of these 
            observers making the <q>correct</q> measurement of the distance between the two events? Which law of 
            physics has something to say about this?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	To formalize the discussion in section 1.4 a bit more, let's define a new quantity <m>w=ct</m> and 
            redefine velocity to be <m>\frac{dx}{dw}</m> instead of <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>. When we make this change, 
            what are the new units of velocity, acceleration, and momentum? What is the conversion factor between 
            <q>regular units</q> and the corresponding <q>Special Relativity</q> (SR) units?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Sections 2.1 and 2.2 spend a lot of time talking about gravity. Look at the definition of inertial 
            reference frame in section 2.3 and explain why our inertial reference frames need to be in free-fall. 
            Is this an issue in an empty universe (i.e., one with no matter)? If you get stuck, it's ok to just
            write down your general thoughts on this.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            The time interval assigned to a pair of events following the procedure of section 2.7 is called the 
            <term>coordinate time interval</term> of an event. In section 1.3 the textbook introduced the 
            <term>wristwatch time</term> as well as the <term>spacetime interval</term>. List a few differences
            between these types of times, i.e, how you might tell if a time interval is a coordinate time, 
            wristwatch time, or spacetime interval. We will have a lot more to say about this, so it's OK if you
            can't find many differences or get stuck.            
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
